// ============================================================================
// Hunt: Microsoft agent-framework PyPI compromise - rogue releases 1.26.1
//   and 1.26.2 carrying a credential stealer, published November 17, 2026
//   through Microsoft's legitimate PyPI publisher account (Wiz)
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1195.002 (Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software
//   Supply Chain), T1552.001 (Credentials in Files), T1550.001
//   (Application Access Token), T1071.001 (Web Protocols), T1041
//   (Exfiltration Over C2 Channel), T1078 (Valid Accounts)
// Data: Microsoft Defender XDR - DeviceFileEvents, DeviceProcessEvents,
//   DeviceNetworkEvents
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-18-2026.html
// Logic: Two malicious releases of Microsoft's official agent-framework
//   Python package appeared on PyPI on the evening of November 17, 2026:
//   1.26.1 at 20:35 UTC and 1.26.2 at 21:08 UTC. Both were removed by
//   about 21:30 UTC and 1.26.0 is again the latest release - but removal
//   from PyPI does not remove copies already installed or cached, and
//   any environment that installed either version during the roughly
//   45-minute window should be treated as compromised. The stealer needs
//   no import to run: each version drops a .pth file into the
//   site-packages root - agent_framework_meta.pth in 1.26.1,
//   af_runtime_diag.pth in 1.26.2 - and Python executes .pth files on
//   every interpreter start, so the collector runs whenever Python starts
//   in that environment. It sweeps environment variables matching a long
//   keyword list (TOKEN, SECRET, KEY, AZURE, AWS, GITHUB, OPENAI, HF_,
//   FOUNDRY, PIP, NPM, NUGET and more), reads credential files such as
//   Azure CLI data, .npmrc, .netrc, .git-credentials, Docker
//   configuration and .env files, and requests fresh Azure DevOps OIDC
//   tokens, Azure managed-identity tokens and - in 1.26.2 - GitHub
//   Actions OIDC tokens for the two audiences Azure federated
//   credentials accept. Everything is sent to a webhook.site address.
//   Wiz, which traced the uploads, said the destination used the
//   service's free tier and that no more than 50 requests were captured
//   - a small number, but the window was short and the package sees
//   about 41,000 downloads a week. The releases came through
//   Microsoft's legitimate PyPI publisher account, not a lookalike
//   name: Wiz found no matching 1.26.1 tag or release in Microsoft's
//   GitHub repository and no malicious code on its main branch, which
//   points to the publishing account rather than the source code. How
//   the attacker obtained publishing access is not yet known, and PyPI
//   has quarantined the publisher - though the attacker is believed to
//   still hold publishing access, since 1.26.2 was published after
//   1.26.1 was pulled. This is the second Microsoft package on PyPI to
//   receive rogue releases in six months. The .pth filenames are the
//   strongest signal: they exist only in the two malicious releases.
//   Variants 3 and 4 are corroboration, not proof on their own -
//   webhook.site is a legitimate service and Python reading a .env
//   file is normal in development. No exfiltration endpoint beyond
//   webhook.site was published, so none is hunted here.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: the malicious .pth persistence files --
//    Each rogue release drops its own .pth file into the site-packages
//    root, and Python executes .pth files on every interpreter start.
//    A hit means the malicious package was installed on this device:
//    treat it as compromised, pin agent-framework==1.26.0, rebuild the
//    environment and rotate every secret the machine could reach,
//    including Azure service principal credentials, GitHub and npm
//    tokens, and anything in .env files. The 14-day window covers the
//    November 17 evening window with room for late-reporting telemetry.
DeviceFileEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(14d)
| where FileName in~ ("agent_framework_meta.pth", "af_runtime_diag.pth")
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, DeviceId, FileName, FolderPath,
    ActionType, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by Timestamp desc

// -- Variant 1b: the same .pth files, scoped to site-packages paths --
//    The stealer drops its .pth file into a site-packages root. If
//    Variant 1 is noisy in your environment, restrict to those paths -
//    but do not drop Variant 1 entirely: a .pth file with either of
//    these names is malicious wherever it sits.
DeviceFileEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(14d)
| where FileName in~ ("agent_framework_meta.pth", "af_runtime_diag.pth")
| where FolderPath has "site-packages"
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, DeviceId, FileName, FolderPath,
    ActionType, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by Timestamp desc

// -- Variant 2: pip installing the two malicious versions --
//    The rogue releases were live for about 45 minutes on the evening
//    of November 17, 2026 (1.26.1 at 20:35 UTC, 1.26.2 at 21:08 UTC).
//    Any install of either version is a compromise indicator, whether
//    it came from pip directly or through python -m pip. The version
//    string is matched as a substring, so also check the full command
//    line on every hit - "1.26.1" would also match a later patch
//    release such as 1.26.10 if one ever ships.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(14d)
| where FileName in~ ("pip.exe", "pip3.exe", "python.exe", "pythonw.exe")
| where ProcessCommandLine has_any ("agent-framework", "agent_framework")
| where ProcessCommandLine has "1.26.1" or ProcessCommandLine has "1.26.2"
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, DeviceId, FileName, ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by Timestamp desc

// -- Variant 2b: any agent-framework install in the November 17 evening
//    window - exposure scoping --
//    The malicious versions were only on PyPI between about 20:35 and
//    21:30 UTC on November 17, so any agent-framework install in that
//    window deserves a check even if the version string is not in the
//    command line (cached wheels and lockfiles can hide it). This is
//    exposure scoping, not a compromise indicator on its own: an
//    install of 1.26.0 in the same window is safe, so confirm the
//    installed version with pip show agent-framework on every hit.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp between (datetime(2026-11-17T20:00:00Z) .. datetime(2026-11-17T22:00:00Z))
| where FileName in~ ("pip.exe", "pip3.exe", "python.exe", "pythonw.exe")
| where ProcessCommandLine has_any ("agent-framework", "agent_framework")
| where ProcessCommandLine has "install"
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, DeviceId, FileName, ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by Timestamp desc

// -- Variant 3: Python reading credential files --
//    The stealer reads Azure CLI data, .npmrc, .netrc, .git-credentials,
//    Docker configuration and .env files. Python touching one of these
//    is normal in development, so this variant is corroboration only:
//    run it on devices that hit Variants 1 or 2, and look for a burst
//    of reads across several of these files in the same Python session
//    - that pattern is the collector sweeping, not a build step.
DeviceFileEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(14d)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("python.exe", "pythonw.exe")
| where FileName in~ (".npmrc", ".netrc", ".git-credentials", ".env")
    or FolderPath has ".azure"
    or (FolderPath has ".docker" and FileName =~ "config.json")
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, DeviceId, FileName, FolderPath,
    ActionType, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by Timestamp desc

// -- Variant 4: Python talking to webhook.site --
//    Everything the stealer collects is sent to a webhook.site address.
//    webhook.site is a legitimate service, so a hit on its own is not
//    proof - but Python reaching it is unusual, and combined with a
//    Variant 1 or 2 hit it confirms the exfiltration path. Correlate
//    the timestamps: exfiltration follows the first Python start after
//    the malicious install.
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(14d)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("python.exe", "pythonw.exe")
| where RemoteUrl has "webhook.site"
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, DeviceId, RemoteUrl, RemoteIP,
    RemotePort, LocalIP, InitiatingProcessFileName,
    InitiatingProcessCommandLine, ActionType
| order by Timestamp desc
